Wanting both love and erotic openness from your wife is a legitimate desire. But the durable version has to be mutual, not “she should want it exactly how I want it.”

What you seem to be reaching for is: I don’t want to be merely desired physically, and I don’t want a marriage that is emotionally loving but sexually closed. I want one relationship where affection, trust, honesty, play, vulnerability, and sexuality all belong together.

On the religious piece, marital intimacy is not automatically “sin” just because it is sexual or adventurous. Different traditions draw different boundaries, but consent, care, fidelity to whatever commitments you two have made, and freedom from coercion matter a lot more than shame-based rules about desire.

The key distinction is this: you can invite openness, create safety for openness, and tell her what you long for—but you cannot manufacture her desire for her. The goal is a shared sexual language that both of you genuinely choose.

And yes, love and lust do not have to compete. In a good marriage, they can reinforce each other: I know you, I trust you, I choose you, and I still want you.